<idea-plugin visibility="internal">
  <!-- @skip-dependency-generation: a discovered test plugin gets no automatic dependency addition, so this is
       declared by hand (see community/python/python-process-output/lambdaTests for the precedent this suite
       copies). A content module's compile classpath (the .iml) is NOT the same graph as this runtime list: a
       class resolvable at compile time can still throw NoClassDefFoundError inside a running lambda if its
       owning module isn't declared here (confirmed by an earlier, now-reverted version of this file that
       omitted `intellij.python.psi.impl` on the theory that "real production modules already part of the
       normal layout" don't need declaring — that is false; a content module's classloader parents come only
       from this list, not from what else happens to be loaded in the process).

       `intellij.python.parser` (`PyLanguageFacade`, `LanguageLevel`) and `intellij.platform.projectModel`
       (`FilePropertyPusher`) are listed because they are used directly, by class reference, inside this suite's
       lambdas, and both are safe to list because both load on *both* sides of a split run — `PyLanguageFacade`
       is implemented on the frontend by `PyFrontendLanguageFacade` and on the backend by `PyLanguageFacadeImpl`,
       and `intellij.platform.projectModel` is depended on directly by frontend modules
       (e.g. `community/python/python-sdk/frontend/intellij.python.sdk.frontend.iml`).

       `intellij.python.psi.impl` (`PythonLanguageLevelPusher`, the real `persistAttribute` implementation) is
       deliberately NOT listed, even though the backend lambda needs its behavior: that module is backend-only
       (absent from the frontend's product layout), and this content module is the *same* module loaded on both
       processes of a split run — a listed dependency unavailable on one side silently excludes the whole module
       from that side (see community/python/python-process-output/lambdaTests/resources/
       intellij.python.processOutput.lambdaTests.xml for the prior precedent of this exact rule). Declaring it
       here would make every `runInFrontend` call fail with "Test plugin with test module ... is not found"
       instead of running this suite's frontend lambda at all. Instead, the backend lambda reaches the real
       pusher dynamically through `FilePropertyPusher.EP_NAME.extensionList`, never naming
       `PythonLanguageLevelPusher` in this module's own bytecode — see the KDoc on
       `PyLanguageFacadeFrontendSyncTest` for the full reasoning. -->
  <dependencies>
    <module name="intellij.lambda.testFramework"/>
    <module name="intellij.python.parser"/>
    <module name="intellij.platform.projectModel"/>
  </dependencies>
</idea-plugin>
